<body>
<h1>Unit 3: Kinematics</h1><p class="meta">Physics XI  |  NEB (Grade 11)  |  Phy. 101 &nbsp;•&nbsp; Content area: Mechanics</p><p class="summary">Kinematics describes how things move without saying why. Its power is the graph: a position-time graph carries velocity, acceleration and everything else inside it, and the constant-acceleration formulas are only a special case of what the graph can show.</p><h2>What the model asserts</h2><ol><li>Kinematics is the description of motion; dynamics is the explanation of motion. Newton's laws belong to the second half of the story.</li><li>v = u + at, s = ut + (1/2)at^2 and v^2 = u^2 + 2as all assume constant acceleration over the interval considered.</li><li>Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity, not of speed: a body moving in a circle at constant speed is accelerating continuously.</li><li>On a position-time graph the slope is velocity, the curvature is acceleration; the area under a velocity-time graph is displacement.</li><li>Near the Earth's surface a body in free fall accelerates at g = 9.8 m/s^2 downward, and the independence from mass holds only when air resistance is negligible.</li><li>The choice of sign convention (up positive or down positive) is a free decision; the physics is unchanged, but the arithmetic changes if you are inconsistent.</li><li>The three equations of motion are not three independent laws: they are one kinematic constraint written three ways, and they hold only for constant acceleration.</li></ol><h2>Conditions, assumptions and limits</h2><ul><li>The constant-acceleration formulas fail for air drag, terminal velocity, appreciable changes in g with altitude, and charged particles in fields.</li><li>A vertical throw and a free fall from rest have identical acceleration-time graphs even though their velocity graphs differ - a standard examination trap.</li><li>Average velocity over a closed round trip is zero while the average speed is not, because the average of a vector quantity is not the average of its magnitude.</li><li>Projectile motion ignores the Earth's curvature and rotation; those corrections matter for long-range artillery and for orbital projectiles, not for a 10 m throw.</li><li>Each equation of motion throws away the shape of the velocity-time graph, so two motions with the same initial and final velocity can still be different - the acceleration history is what gets lost.</li></ul><h2>Key numbers</h2><table><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>Standard gravity</td><td>9.80665 m/s^2</td></tr><tr><td>Typical terminal speed of a skydiver</td><td>about 55 m/s</td></tr><tr><td>Speed of sound in air at 20 degrees C</td><td>about 343 m/s</td></tr></table><h2>Open questions and mysteries</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>How precisely can the equivalence of free fall be tested as the mass of the test body is reduced?</b></p><p class="status">Under active scrutiny</p><p>Drop-tower and lunar/planetary drop experiments push the test to ever smaller masses; any quantum-gravity signal would appear as a deviation from the predicted free-fall trajectory.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>Does a trajectory carry more information than any observer can extract from it, given that measuring position disturbs the motion?</b></p><p class="status">Open question</p><p>Quantum limits on phase-space resolution remain an active research topic, and the classical description hides the disturbance entirely.</p></div><h2>Inquiry prompts</h2><ol><li>Derive v^2 = u^2 + 2as from the definition of average velocity, stating at each step where constant acceleration was used.</li><li>Sketch x-t, v-t and a-t graphs for a ball thrown vertically upward and mark every sign change.</li><li>A car travelling at 20 m/s brakes to rest in 2.5 s: find the retardation, then the reaction distance needed to avoid a collision.</li></ol><h2>Further reading</h2><ul><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/preface">OpenStax: University Physics Volume 1</a></li><li><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/dark-matter/">NASA: Dark Matter</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/3-4-motion-with-constant-acceleration">OpenStax: Motion with Constant Acceleration</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/3-5-free-fall">OpenStax: Free Fall</a></li></ul><p class="note">Physics XI — Academic Companion — a companion brief, not a replacement for the prescribed NEB text. The open questions describe live research frontiers; they are enrichment, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed text and syllabus for examinable scope.</p>
</body>
